 MODULATION CHEATSHEET                              4 of 100 entries
 pivot-chord modulations in four voices                68 col, ASCII
====================================================================

 HOW TO USE
   Sections 1-3 are reference; read once. Section 4 is the index.
   Section 5 holds the entries. Section 6 is the blank template.

   Every entry is a table plus a one-line summary. The table is
   authoritative; the line is generated from it. If they ever
   disagree, the table is right.

   Grep: "^ A2" jumps to an entry. "TODO" finds unfinished work.


====================================================================
 1.  THE LINE
====================================================================

  A2  C → D    Iᶜ → iii′ᶜ | ii′ᵈ → Vᵈ ⇒ Iᵈ
  ──  ─────    ──────────────────────────────
  id  keys     the progression

  Read left to right, one symbol per sounding chord — except at
  the pivot, where two symbols share one chord. Symbol count
  therefore exceeds event count by exactly one.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
 CHORD SYMBOLS
--------------------------------------------------------------------
  I  IV  V      UPPERCASE numeral = MAJOR triad
  ii iii vi     lowercase numeral = MINOR triad

  Case carries quality and is never optional. The source writes
  every numeral uppercase, concealing quality; correcting the
  case is the first edit when transcribing. A numeral not yet
  checked against its pitches is not yet transcribed.

  The numeral counts degrees in the key given by ITS OWN raised
  key letter — not the entry's key, not the preceding chord's.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
 RAISED KEY LETTER        ᶜ ᵈ ᵉ ᵍ ᵃ …
--------------------------------------------------------------------
  Every chord carries, raised and immediately after the numeral,
  the key it is read in.

      iii′ᶜ     "third degree, first inversion, read in C"

  Numeral first, key second. The CHORD is the object under
  discussion; the key is the vantage point. Writing the key
  first (the source's CIII) makes the key the possessor and the
  chord its property — the wrong relation, since a pivot chord
  belongs to no key exclusively.

  Key letters are always lowercase whatever the mode; the entry
  header determines major or minor. This is a separate namespace
  from chord case, deliberately, so a raised letter is never
  mistaken for a quality mark.

  The letter repeats on every chord, including runs in one key.
  Redundant by design: any fragment stays readable when quoted
  alone, and the pivot marks can be checked against the letters
  rather than trusted.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
 → MOTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------
  Separates two DIFFERENT sounding chords. Ordinary succession;
  no analytic claim beyond order.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
 | PIVOT, DIATONIC              the reinterpretation is mutual
--------------------------------------------------------------------
      iii′ᶜ | ii′ᵈ

  ONE sounding chord written twice. Nothing moves across the
  bar; the same pitches are held or restruck while the ear's
  reading of them changes. Left of the bar is the departing key,
  right of it the arriving key.

  The bar also partitions the line: nothing left of it is in the
  new key, nothing right of it in the old. The raised letters
  state this independently, so the two check each other.

  The bar asserts BOTH readings are diatonic — neither needs an
  accidental or a borrowing. The pivot is therefore REVERSIBLE:
  the same chord would modulate the other direction unaltered.

  Bar pivots reach one or two fifths. See DIAGNOSTICS.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
 ~ PIVOT, BORROWED             the reinterpretation is one-way
--------------------------------------------------------------------
      iiᶜ ~ ivᵃ

  Also one chord written twice, identical to the bar in every
  respect but one: the right-hand reading is NOT diatonic in its
  key. It is borrowed from that key's parallel minor.

  In the example, D minor is native to C major. Read as the
  fourth degree of A major it needs F natural where A major
  wants F sharp. The chord is a native of the old key and an
  import into the new one.

  The consequence is asymmetry. A major does not offer this
  chord natively, so the return trip requires an alteration the
  outbound trip did not. Bar pivots are reversible; tilde pivots
  are not.

  What the borrowing buys is distance: three or four fifths
  against the bar's one or two. Widening the destination key's
  vocabulary to admit its parallel minor pays for the ground.

  No accidental appears at the tilde, because the borrowed tone
  is the natural form in the departing key. The first accidental
  arrives later, in the dominant.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
 ⇒ CADENTIAL SIX-FOUR
--------------------------------------------------------------------
      Vᵈ ⇒ Iᵈ

  Marks the dominant as bearing a cadential six-four: the sixth
  and fourth above the bass sound first and fall to the fifth
  and third while the bass is held. ONE harmonic event across
  two sonorities.

  What actually sounds is the TONIC triad over the dominant
  bass, arriving two events early and displaced at the next
  event by the true dominant. The CHORD column shows this (D/A
  then A, resolving to D); the line compresses it to ⇒.

  The source writes it as two comma-separated chords ("DV⁶₄,
  DV"), which is the second most common transcription error
  after uppercase numerals. Joining them is a required edit.

  METRE, not otherwise notated: the six-four takes the stronger
  beat, its resolution the weaker. Reversed it becomes a passing
  chord and the cadence collapses. All four entries observe it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
 ′  ″  INVERSION
--------------------------------------------------------------------
  none   root position        Iᶜ    = C E G,  bass C
   ′     first inversion      iii′ᶜ = E G B,  bass G
   ″     second inversion     V″ᶜ   = D G B,  bass D

  prime   figure   bass
  -----   ------   --------
  none     ⁵₃      root
   ′       ⁶ (⁶₃)  third
   ″       ⁶₄      fifth

  Figures name intervals above the bass; primes count
  inversions. For TRIADS they always agree.

  SEVENTH CHORDS BREAK THIS. Four positions, and the figures
  stop counting evenly:
      root ⁷ (⁷₅₃)   third ⁶₅   fifth ⁴₃   seventh ⁴₂ (²)
  A third prime would have to mean ⁴₃ and a fourth ⁴₂, which no
  reader will infer. TRIADS ONLY — if a seventh enters the
  collection, that entry reverts to figures and says so.

  ⇒ IS NOT AN INVERSION. It is figured ⁶₄ but is a suspension
  over a held dominant bass, not a second-inversion tonic.
  Never written V″.

  A prime appears only where the inversion is analytically
  significant. Its presence is itself a signal: if you see one,
  look at the bass.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
 WHAT IS NOT NOTATED
--------------------------------------------------------------------
  Voicing, doubling, spacing, register, distribution among the
  four parts. Rhythm. Metre beyond the six-four convention.
  Anything the source adds outside the five-event frame.
  These belong with the score. The line encodes the harmonic
  argument only.


