  t         time index; one row per sounding event
  CHORD     the SONORITY — the keystone. Read this column down.
            Slash form is chord-over-bass: D/A is a D major
            triad with A in the bass, NOT an A chord.
  pitches   its tones, bass first
  in X      how key X reads the sonority. Both filled = pivot.
            ·  key not yet entered
            ✗  foreclosed — a tone foreign to that key has
               sounded. Permanent; a foreclosed key never
               returns, so the ✗ run is unbroken to the end.
  *         appended to a reading: borrowed. Diatonic in the old
            key, chromatic in the new. Marks a ~ pivot.
  comment   free text. What the row DOES, not what it is — the
            other columns already say what it is. Keep it prose;
            if it starts accruing abbreviations it has become a
            second notation to learn.


====================================================================
 3.  MECHANISMS AND DIAGNOSTICS
====================================================================

  Distance in fifths determines the pivot type:

    ±1   old tonic itself is the pivot          |    diatonic
    ±2   relative minor of the chord a fifth    |    diatonic
         above (or below) the old tonic
    3-4  a minor triad of the old key reheard   ~    borrowed
         as MINOR SUBDOMINANT of the new
     0   relative keys; any shared chord        |    free choice

  For the 3-4 case: a minor triad on X is the minor subdominant
  of the major key a fifth BELOW X.
        d min = ii  in C  →  iv of A   (+3)
        a min = vi  in C  →  iv of E   (+4)
        e min = iii in C  →  iv of B   (+5)

  Beyond +5 expect a chain of pivots.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
 CHECKS
--------------------------------------------------------------------
  MARK vs DISTANCE. A bar should sit at 1-2 fifths, a tilde at
  3-4. A bar at four fifths, or a tilde at one, means the pivot
  was misread — usually a borrowed reading taken as diatonic.

  COUNT THE RAISED LETTERS. A line with a single letter of the
  departing key has no real departure: it opens on its own pivot
  and never establishes the key it claims to leave.

  GAP. The pivot and the point of no return are not always the
  same event. Gap = rows from the pivot to the first ✗. A gap of
  1 is decisive. A larger gap means the named pivot did little
  and a later chord did the work.

  TWO KINDS OF ⁶₄. The source's ⁶₄ is sometimes an inversion
  (becomes ′ or ″) and sometimes the cadential suspension
  (becomes ⇒). They are written identically and mean unrelated
  things. The cadential one always sits on the dominant
  immediately before the final tonic.


